# Calling your API

When your app serves an OpenAPI document with
[`openapi.serve`](/docs/openapi/overview), the admin finds it on its own and
adds **API → Endpoints** to the sidebar. There is nothing to register:
`admin.mount` looks through the app's routes for the documents
`howdy_openapi` serves, so mount the admin after `openapi.serve`.

```gleam
my_app.app(db, identity)   // ends with openapi.serve(spec, at: "/openapi.json")
|> admin.mount(admin.new() |> admin.auth(identity))
```

## Endpoints

The endpoints page lists every operation in the document, grouped by tag,
with the ones that need authentication marked. With a
[version group](/docs/middleware/versioning), each version has its own list;
the links at the top switch between them.

The admin reads the document as JSON, the way any client would, so what it
shows is what the document says.

## Calling an endpoint

Each endpoint's page has a form built from the document:

- A field for each path, query and header parameter, marked when it is
  required. A parameter that can only take one value, such as a version
  header, starts filled in. Array query parameters take values separated by
  commas.
- A JSON body, started from an example made from the body's schema.
- A field for each security scheme the endpoint requires: a bearer token,
  or an API key in a header, cookie or query parameter.
- **Send as**, when `admin.auth` is registered.

**Send request** runs the request through the app in the same process, past
its middleware and guards exactly as a request from outside would go, and
shows the response: its status, how long it took, the body pretty-printed,
the headers, and the same request as a `curl` command against the server.

Below the form are the documented responses, and the fields of the schemas
they and the body use.

## Calling as a user

To call an endpoint behind `auth.required` without knowing anyone's
password, choose a user under **Send as**. The admin opens a session for
them with `auth.impersonate`, the same way **Sign in as** on a user's page
does, and sends its token as `authorization: Bearer <token>`. Your guard
sees a real session for that user, so permission checks apply exactly as
they would for them.

The session is kept for later calls as the same user, so calling again does
not open another. If it is revoked or expires, the admin opens a new one
when the app answers `401`. Each session appears on the user's page with
the method `impersonation`, and the audit trail records it.

Your browser's own cookies are never sent, so being signed in to the app in
another tab makes no difference: a call is anonymous unless you choose a
user or type a credential.
